The rules in rules/ are copied from scb-check 0.2.0
(https://github.com/gabeorlanski/scb-check, tag v0.2.0, commit
f808b0fb214501092ae3c7d914dfe98fe2a312af), from
src/scb_check/resources/slop_rules/. Upstream is not tracked.

Changes from the release: each rule is in a file of its own, named for its
id, and the rule tests in tests/ are the standards' own. So are the messages
of conditional-same-target-subscript-assign, manual-str-join,
repetitive-list-append-elif, manual-dict-counter-if-else,
manual-set-union-loop, verbose-missing-remove-loop and
init-populate-iterate-dict, rewritten to advice the standard allows, and the
rule body of dict-get-empty-string-default, which matches an assignment
statement where the release's never matched. Every rule has severity warning
and no metadata.weight, since neither decides whether a rule gates.

Dropped since they were copied: consecutive-append-calls,
set-add-loop and dict-from-pairs-loop, which Ruff reads as FURB113, FURB142
and PERF403; wrap-raise-fstring, which Ruff reads as EM102; and
is-true-false-strict and isinstance-bool-exclusion, whose advice is wrong
for strictly typed parsed data.
